# stored = { held: one }; stored.__proto__ = somethingNoTableLists; stored.label
# A store under `__proto__` runs the setter of the table, so an object nobody knows lies above the object from then on. It may have a getter under any name that the object lacks, and that getter is handed the object as `this`:
# the read lets the object escape, and what it holds is entered. one-receiver-with-an-unknown-getter-escapes-alone.ir has the same with `__proto__` in a literal.
# The setter of the table is applied once for all the stores of a program that find it, with the receivers and the values of all of them. So the store of an object of the program has a file of its own:
# known-prototype-stored-by-name-holds-no-accessor.ir
# RUN: valueflow-solve unreached
# CHECK-NOT: unreached f1

module m0 path="main.js" displayPath="main.js" body=f0
entry module=m0
layout L0 name="StoredByName" fields=[{key="held" declared={type=anything} flags=[IsRequired]}]
function f0 name="main" kind=Module results=[{type=anything}] {
  b0():
    v0 = NewClosure function=f1
    v1 = NewObject -, v0 layout=L0 fields=[0]
    v2 = Builtin builtin=GlobalThis
    v3 = PropGet v2 key="somethingThatNoTableLists" [isFreeName]
    PropSet v1, v3 key="__proto__" mode=Strict
    v5 = PropGet v1 key="label"
    Return
}
function f1 name="one" results=[{type=anything}] {
  b0():
    Return
}
